// @check
// @error a rest parameter's element is inferred from the arguments, and these join to i32 | string — a boxed union a type parameter cannot be pinned to. Spell the element out, `...xs: (i32 | string)[]`, or pass one element type
// @error a rest parameter's element is inferred from the arguments, and these join to boolean | string — a boxed union a type parameter cannot be pinned to. Spell the element out, `...xs: (boolean | string)[]`, or pass one element type
// @error a rest parameter's element is inferred from the arguments, and these join to {a: i32} | {b: i32} — a boxed union a type parameter cannot be pinned to. Spell the element out, `...xs: ({a: i32} | {b: i32})[]`, or pass one element type
// A rest parameter's element HOLE cannot pin to a union that BOXES. Refused at the pin, and
// the sentence names the concrete spelling that runs — which is the point of refusing here
// rather than leaving it to the monomorphizer, whose instance for such a pin builds a module
// that does not validate. `(i32 | boolean)` is NOT refused: both arms are i32-repped, so it
// rides the spine and runs, and its running twin is `rest-element-hole-joins.vl`.
type A = { a: i32 }
type B = { b: i32 }
function f<T>(...xs: T[]) { return xs.length }
print(f(1, "x"))
print(f(true, "x"))
print(f({ a: 1 }, { b: 2 }))
